## Feedback Loops (binding across All phases)

### 1. Parity Checks

Every from-scratch implementation is compared against a known reference: linear/logistic vs scikit-learn (numeric, pytest), autograd vs PyTorch, attention output vs a PyTorch reference, retrieval vs keyword baseline, ONNX inference vs native Python inference.

### 2. Teach-back Documents

One written explanation per phase, working-note quality by default:

| Phase | Teach-back |
|-------|-----------|
| 1 | How supervised tabular ML works |
| 2 | How backpropagation works |
| 3 | How transformer attention works |
| 4 | Why retrieval quality controls RAG quality |
| 5 | Local inference tradeoffs |
| 6 | When fine-tuning is worth it |
| 7 | How Sailfish deploys local ML safely |

### 3. Evaluation before Generation

For RAG and LLM systems: eval questions, expected sources, retrieval metrics, answer evaluation, then app interface. In that order.

### 4. Spaced Re-derivation

At the start of each phase, re-derive one key concept from the prior phase, from a blank page:

| Before starting | Re-derive |
|-----------------|-----------|
| Phase 2 | Logistic regression loss and gradient |
| Phase 3 | Backprop through a simple MLP |
| Phase 4 | Q/K/V attention |
| Phase 5 | Embedding similarity and recall@k |
| Phase 6 | Transformer fine-tuning objective |
| Phase 7 | End-to-end model lifecycle |

### 5. Deployment Reality Checks

Every phase after Phase 1 answers: Could this become part of a Sailfish Business Hub or service offering? If yes, what is the deployment boundary? If no, what did I learn that still matters?

